start=3D"4585" data-end=3D"4603">(channels, H, W)</code></p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4606" data-end=3D"4641">
<p data-start=3D"4608" data-end=3D"4641">Output: <code data-start=3D"4616" =
data-end=3D"4641">(requested_hours, H, W)</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"4643" data-end=3D"4646">
<h2 data-start=3D"4648" data-end=3D"4696">4. What about the <code data-star=
t=3D"4669" data-end=3D"4675">.npy</code> files you mentioned?</h2>
<p data-start=3D"4698" data-end=3D"4734">Those 12 <code data-start=3D"4707"=
 data-end=3D"4713">.npy</code> files from Zeus are:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"4736" data-end=3D"5025">
<li data-start=3D"4736" data-end=3D"4811">
<p data-start=3D"4738" data-end=3D"4811">precomputed grids, difficulty weig=
hts, masks, maybe baseline arrays, etc.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4812" data-end=3D"4866">
<p data-start=3D"4814" data-end=3D"4866">used mainly by the <strong data-st=
art=3D"4833" data-end=3D"4846">validator</strong> and some utilities.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4867" data-end=3D"4932">
<p data-start=3D"4869" data-end=3D"4932">They are <strong data-start=3D"487=
8" data-end=3D"4894">not required</strong> to understand your miner source =
code.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4933" data-end=3D"5025">
<p data-start=3D"4935" data-end=3D"5025">They are <strong data-start=3D"494=
4" data-end=3D"4974">not raw ERA5 training data</strong>, so they=E2=80=99r=
e not enough to fully train an ML model.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"5027" data-end=3D"5188">For <strong data-start=3D"5031" da=
ta-end=3D"5050">proper training</strong>, we should use <strong data-start=
=3D"5066" data-end=3D"5090">ERA5 reanalysis data</strong> (via CDS or Googl=
e Cloud), then <em data-start=3D"5123" data-end=3D"5135">optionally</em> al=
ign some parts with those <code data-start=3D"5164" data-end=3D"5170">.npy<=
/code> structures later.</p>
<p data-start=3D"5190" data-end=3D"5270">So yes: reviewing the source first=
 (what we just did) is exactly the right step.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"5272" data-end=3D"5275">
<h2 data-start=3D"5277" data-end=3D"5336">5. Next step: designing the train=
ing script (high-level)</h2>
<p data-start=3D"5338" data-end=3D"5355">Now that we know:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"5357" data-end=3D"5572">
<li data-start=3D"5357" data-end=3D"5415">
<p data-start=3D"5359" data-end=3D"5415">The miner needs to output <code da=
ta-start=3D"5385" data-end=3D"5396">(T, H, W)</code> in <strong data-start=
=3D"5400" data-end=3D"5414">ERA5 units</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5416" data-end=3D"5488">
<p data-start=3D"5418" data-end=3D"5488">Variable is <strong data-start=3D"=
5430" data-end=3D"5447">one at a time</strong> (dewpoint, u-wind, v-wind, p=
recip, etc.)</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5489" data-end=3D"5572">
<p data-start=3D"5491" data-end=3D"5572">Grid is <strong data-start=3D"5499=
" data-end=3D"5512">arbitrary</strong> (different H and W each call) but co=
ordinates are on Earth.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"5574" data-end=3D"5632">We can design a first <strong data=
-start=3D"5596" data-end=3D"5621">simple training setup</strong> like this:=
</p>
<ol data-start=3D"5634" data-end=3D"6104">
<li data-start=3D"5634" data-end=3D"5709">
<p data-start=3D"5637" data-end=3D"5709"><strong data-start=3D"5637" data-e=
nd=3D"5654">Download ERA5</strong> for a region (or entire globe at coarse =
resolution).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5710" data-end=3D"5849">
<p data-start=3D"5713" data-end=3D"5732">Build a dataset of:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"5736" data-end=3D"5849">
<li data-start=3D"5736" data-end=3D"5785">
<p data-start=3D"5738" data-end=3D"5785">Input: past <code data-start=3D"57=
50" data-end=3D"5753">N</code> hours of a variable on a grid</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5789" data-end=3D"5849">
<p data-start=3D"5791" data-end=3D"5849">Target: next <code data-start=3D"5=
804" data-end=3D"5807">T</code> hours of that variable on the same grid</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5850" data-end=3D"5986">
<p data-start=3D"5853" data-end=3D"5892">Train a small <strong data-start=
=3D"5867" data-end=3D"5886">U-Net / ConvNet</strong> that:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"5896" data-end=3D"5986">
<li data-start=3D"5896" data-end=3D"5936">
<p data-start=3D"5898" data-end=3D"5936">Input shape: <code data-start=3D"5=
911" data-end=3D"5934">(context_hours, H, W)</code></p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5940" data-end=3D"5986">
<p data-start=3D"5942" data-end=3D"5986">Output shape: <code data-start=3D"=
5956" data-end=3D"5984">(max_forecast_hours, H, W)</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5987" data-end=3D"6104">
<p data-start=3D"5990" data-end=3D"6003">In the miner:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"6007" data-end=3D"6104">
<li data-start=3D"6007" data-end=3D"6104">
<p data-start=3D"6009" data-end=3D"6104">When Zeus asks for <code data-star=
t=3D"6028" data-end=3D"6045">requested_hours</code>, you slice the model=E2=
=80=99s output <code data-start=3D"6076" data-end=3D"6103">[0:requested_hou=
rs, :, :]</code>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start=3D"6106" data-end=3D"6222">Because your logs show small grids=
 like <code data-start=3D"6146" data-end=3D"6155">(11, 9)</code>, <code dat=
a-start=3D"6157" data-end=3D"6166">(7, 13)</code>, <code data-start=3D"6168=
" data-end=3D"6178">(13, 12)</code>, a CNN over those grids is very reasona=
ble.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"6224" data-end=3D"6227">
<h2 data-start=3D"6229" data-end=3D"6260">6. What I=E2=80=99ll do next for =
you</h2>
<p data-start=3D"6262" data-end=3D"6368">Since you can=E2=80=99t share the =
big <code data-start=3D"6292" data-end=3D"6298">.npy</code> files and we ha=
ve the miner interface already, I=E2=80=99ll in the next step:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"6370" data-end=3D"6835">
<li data-start=3D"6370" data-end=3D"6667">
<p data-start=3D"6372" data-end=3D"6407">Sketch a <strong data-start=3D"638=
1" data-end=3D"6401"><code data-start=3D"6383" data-end=3D"6399">train_mode=
l.py</code></strong> that:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"6410" data-end=3D"6667">
