ger batch size =3D faster training per epoch</strong>,<br data-start=3D"70"=
 data-end=3D"73">
but <strong data-start=3D"77" data-end=3D"150">NO =E2=80=94 it doesn=E2=80=
=99t always mean faster total training OR better accuracy.</strong></p>
<p data-start=3D"152" data-end=3D"219">Let me explain it clearly, especiall=
y for your RTX 4090 Zeus model.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"221" data-end=3D"224">
<h1 data-start=3D"226" data-end=3D"271">=E2=9C=85 <strong data-start=3D"230=
" data-end=3D"271">How Batch Size Affects Training Speed</strong></h1>
<p data-start=3D"272" data-end=3D"292">Here=E2=80=99s what happens:</p>
<h3 data-start=3D"294" data-end=3D"346">=E2=9C=94 <strong data-start=3D"300=
" data-end=3D"346">Bigger batch size =E2=86=92 better GPU utilization</stro=
ng></h3>
<p data-start=3D"347" data-end=3D"412">4090 loves big matrix operations.<br=
 data-start=3D"380" data-end=3D"383">
When you increase batch size:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"414" data-end=3D"606">
<li data-start=3D"414" data-end=3D"466">
<p data-start=3D"416" data-end=3D"466">More data processed in one forward/b=
ackward pass</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"467" data-end=3D"492">
<p data-start=3D"469" data-end=3D"492">GPU stays busy longer</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"493" data-end=3D"535">
<p data-start=3D"495" data-end=3D"535">Less overhead from Python + dataload=
er</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"536" data-end=3D"583">
<p data-start=3D"538" data-end=3D"583">Training <em data-start=3D"547" data=
-end=3D"559">iterations</em> (steps) become faster</p>
