</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"608" data-end=3D"615">So yes:</p>
<h3 data-start=3D"617" data-end=3D"671">=F0=9F=91=89 <strong data-start=3D"=
624" data-end=3D"671">Batch size =E2=86=91 =E2=86=92 Training per epoch is =
faster</strong></h3>
<hr data-start=3D"673" data-end=3D"676">
<h1 data-start=3D"678" data-end=3D"723">=E2=9D=8C But bigger batch is NOT a=
lways =E2=80=9Cbetter=E2=80=9D</h1>
<p data-start=3D"724" data-end=3D"747">Here are the downsides:</p>
<h3 data-start=3D"749" data-end=3D"783"><strong data-start=3D"753" data-end=
=3D"783">1. GPU Out-Of-Memory (OOM)</strong></h3>
<p data-start=3D"784" data-end=3D"809">You already hit OOM with:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"811" data-end=3D"848">
<li data-start=3D"811" data-end=3D"834">
<p data-start=3D"813" data-end=3D"834">batch=3D2 and base=3D64</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"835" data-end=3D"848">
<p data-start=3D"837" data-end=3D"848">~23 GB used</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"850" data-end=3D"897">Increasing batch further will <stron=
g data-start=3D"880" data-end=3D"896">explode VRAM</strong>.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"899" data-end=3D"902">
<h3 data-start=3D"904" data-end=3D"940"><strong data-start=3D"908" data-end=
=3D"940">2. Convergence becomes worse</strong></h3>
<p data-start=3D"941" data-end=3D"957">Too large batch:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"959" data-end=3D"1047">
<li data-start=3D"959" data-end=3D"981">
<p data-start=3D"961" data-end=3D"981">makes model =E2=80=9Clazy=E2=80=9D</=
p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"982" data-end=3D"1012">
<p data-start=3D"984" data-end=3D"1012">causes poor generalization</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1013" data-end=3D"1047">
<p data-start=3D"1015" data-end=3D"1047">optimizer finds shallow minima</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"1049" data-end=3D"1085">This is well-known in deep learnin=
g.</p>
<p data-start=3D"1087" data-end=3D"1111">For Zeus weather models:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"1113" data-end=3D"1198">
<li data-start=3D"1113" data-end=3D"1160">
<p data-start=3D"1115" data-end=3D"1160"><strong data-start=3D"1115" data-e=
nd=3D"1131">batch=3D1 or 2</strong> usually gives best results</p>
