n prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words light markdown-new-styling"><p=
 data-start=3D"0" data-end=3D"157">Nice upgrade =F0=9F=98=84 Short answer: =
<strong data-start=3D"30" data-end=3D"157">an H100 SXM will be a lot faster=
 than your 4090, but not magically 10=C3=97. Realistic is ~2=E2=80=934=C3=
=97 speed-up for your kind of model.</strong></p>
<p data-start=3D"159" data-end=3D"199">Let me break it down in practical te=
rms.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"201" data-end=3D"204">
<h2 data-start=3D"206" data-end=3D"255">1. Raw GPU muscle: H100 vs 4090 (fo=
r training)</h2>
<p data-start=3D"257" data-end=3D"306">Very roughly (for training dtypes we=
 care about):</p>
<ul data-start=3D"308" data-end=3D"659">
<li data-start=3D"308" data-end=3D"477">
<p data-start=3D"310" data-end=3D"327"><strong data-start=3D"310" data-end=
=3D"327">RTX 4090 24GB</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"330" data-end=3D"477">
<li data-start=3D"330" data-end=3D"385">
<p data-start=3D"332" data-end=3D"385">~330 TFLOPs FP16/FP8 (tensor cores, =
marketing number)</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"388" data-end=3D"400">
<p data-start=3D"390" data-end=3D"400">24 GB VRAM</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"403" data-end=3D"477">
<p data-start=3D"405" data-end=3D"477">Gaming-oriented; great, but not opti=
mized for big-batch server training.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"479" data-end=3D"659">
<p data-start=3D"481" data-end=3D"498"><strong data-start=3D"481" data-end=
=3D"498">H100 SXM 80GB</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"501" data-end=3D"659">
<li data-start=3D"501" data-end=3D"565">
<p data-start=3D"503" data-end=3D"565">Much higher effective tensor-core th=
roughput for FP8/FP16/TF32</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"568" data-end=3D"599">
<p data-start=3D"570" data-end=3D"599">80 GB HBM3 (huge + very fast)</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"602" data-end=3D"659">
<p data-start=3D"604" data-end=3D"659">Server card designed for large deep =
learning workloads.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"661" data-end=3D"801">For <em data-start=3D"665" data-end=
=3D"671">your</em> workload (UNet on 0.5=C2=B0 ERA5 grids, batch size curre=
ntly limited by 24 GB), the <strong data-start=3D"751" data-end=3D"783">big=
gest win is actually VRAM</strong>, not just TFLOPs:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"803" data-end=3D"998">
<li data-start=3D"803" data-end=3D"886">
<p data-start=3D"805" data-end=3D"886">On 4090 you=E2=80=99re stuck around =
<strong data-start=3D"833" data-end=3D"846">batch=3D1=E2=80=932</strong> an=
d base channels limited to avoid OOM.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"887" data-end=3D"998">
<p data-start=3D"889" data-end=3D"998">On H100 80GB you=E2=80=99ll comforta=
bly run <strong data-start=3D"925" data-end=3D"939">batch=3D8=E2=80=9316</s=
trong> with a <em data-start=3D"947" data-end=3D"955">bigger</em> model (mo=
re channels, deeper) if you want.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"1000" data-end=3D"1003">
<h2 data-start=3D"1005" data-end=3D"1048">2. What does that mean in trainin=
g time?</h2>
<p data-start=3D"1050" data-end=3D"1094">Right now with 4090 you=E2=80=99ve=
 seen things like:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"1096" data-end=3D"1236">
<li data-start=3D"1096" data-end=3D"1148">
<p data-start=3D"1098" data-end=3D"1148">Precip model, 0.5=C2=B0 grid, cont=
ext 48, forecast 24</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1149" data-end=3D"1162">
<p data-start=3D"1151" data-end=3D"1162">Deep UNet</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1163" data-end=3D"1179">
<p data-start=3D"1165" data-end=3D"1179">Batch size 2</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1180" data-end=3D"1236">
<p data-start=3D"1182" data-end=3D"1236"><strong data-start=3D"1182" data-e=
nd=3D"1210">~50=E2=80=9360 minutes per epoch</strong> (ballpark from your l=
ogs)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"1238" data-end=3D"1264">On <strong data-start=3D"1241" dat=
a-end=3D"1253">H100 SXM</strong>, you gain:</p>
<ol data-start=3D"1266" data-end=3D"1434">
<li data-start=3D"1266" data-end=3D"1353">
<p data-start=3D"1269" data-end=3D"1353"><strong data-start=3D"1269" data-e=
nd=3D"1290">Larger batch size</strong> =E2=86=92 fewer steps per epoch (e.g=
., batch 8 vs 2 =3D 4=C3=97 fewer steps).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1354" data-end=3D"1434">
<p data-start=3D"1357" data-end=3D"1434"><strong data-start=3D"1357" data-e=
nd=3D"1387">Higher throughput per step</strong> due to better tensor cores =
/ memory bandwidth.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start=3D"1436" data-end=3D"1528">Realistic, <strong data-start=3D"1=
447" data-end=3D"1501">for the same model and same =E2=80=9Ceffective=E2=80=
=9D batch size</strong>, you=E2=80=99d see something like:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"1530" data-end=3D"1659">
<li data-start=3D"1530" data-end=3D"1575">
<p data-start=3D"1532" data-end=3D"1575"><strong data-start=3D"1532" data-e=
nd=3D"1556">2=E2=80=933=C3=97 faster per step</strong> just from hardware</=
p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1576" data-end=3D"1659">
<p data-start=3D"1578" data-end=3D"1659"><strong data-start=3D"1578" data-e=
nd=3D"1586">Plus</strong> your ability to increase batch size 2=E2=86=928, =
so <strong data-start=3D"1631" data-end=3D"1659">4=C3=97 fewer steps per ep=
och</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"1661" data-end=3D"1763">But you usually trade some of that=
 extra VRAM for a bigger model, so in practice people end up around:</p>
<blockquote data-start=3D"1765" data-end=3D"1830">
<p data-start=3D"1767" data-end=3D"1830">=F0=9F=91=89 <strong data-start=3D=
"1770" data-end=3D"1797">~2=E2=80=934=C3=97 faster epoch time</strong> for =
a similar or stronger model.</p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start=3D"1832" data-end=3D"1913">So if one epoch currently takes ~5=
0 minutes on 4090, expect <strong data-start=3D"1892" data-end=3D"1912">som=
ewhere around</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"1915" data-end=3D"1983">
<li data-start=3D"1915" data-end=3D"1983">
<p data-start=3D"1917" data-end=3D"1983"><strong data-start=3D"1917" data-e=
nd=3D"1944">15=E2=80=9325 minutes per epoch</strong> on H100 for a comparab=
ly strong model.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"1985" data-end=3D"2115">If you aggressively keep the model=
 size the same and just max out batch size, you could get closer to the upp=
er end of that range.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"2117" data-end=3D"2120">
<h2 data-start=3D"2122" data-end=3D"2162">3. What you actually gain in prac=
tice</h2>
<p data-start=3D"2164" data-end=3D"2189">With an H100 SXM you can:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"2191" data-end=3D"2468">
<li data-start=3D"2191" data-end=3D"2251">
<p data-start=3D"2193" data-end=3D"2251">Train <strong data-start=3D"2199" =
data-end=3D"2224">more hours of context</strong> (e.g. 72=E2=80=9396h) with=
out OOM.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2252" data-end=3D"2325">
<p data-start=3D"2254" data-end=3D"2325">Use <strong data-start=3D"2258" da=
ta-end=3D"2282">larger base channels</strong> (e.g. 96=E2=80=93128) for mor=
e accurate forecasts.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2326" data-end=3D"2386">
<p data-start=3D"2328" data-end=3D"2386">Increase <strong data-start=3D"233=
7" data-end=3D"2351">batch size</strong> to 8=E2=80=9316 for more stable gr=
adients.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2387" data-end=3D"2468">
<p data-start=3D"2389" data-end=3D"2468">Run <strong data-start=3D"2393" da=
ta-end=3D"2427">multiple variables in parallel</strong> (e.g. precip + t2m =
together) on one GPU.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"2470" data-end=3D"2575">So it=E2=80=99s not only <em data-=
start=3D"2487" data-end=3D"2512">=E2=80=9Csame model but faster=E2=80=9D</e=
m> =E2=80=94 it=E2=80=99s also <em data-start=3D"2525" data-end=3D"2574">=
=E2=80=9Cbetter model in roughly the same or less time=E2=80=9D</em>.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"2577" data-end=3D"2580">
<h2 data-start=3D"2582" data-end=3D"2610">4. How I=E2=80=99d plan with H100=
</h2>
<p data-start=3D"2612" data-end=3D"2662">If your goal is <strong data-start=
=3D"2628" data-end=3D"2653">Zeus SN18 performance</strong>, I=E2=80=99d do:=
</p>
<ul data-start=3D"2664" data-end=3D"2927">
<li data-start=3D"2664" data-end=3D"2733">
<p data-start=3D"2666" data-end=3D"2733">Start with current architecture &a=
mp; hyperparams =E2=86=92 measure epoch time.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2734" data-end=3D"2849">
<p data-start=3D"2736" data-end=3D"2746">Then bump:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"2749" data-end=3D"2849">
<li data-start=3D"2749" data-end=3D"2795">
<p data-start=3D"2751" data-end=3D"2795"><code data-start=3D"2751" data-end=
=3D"2763">batch_size</code> up until GPU hits ~80=E2=80=9390% VRAM.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2798" data-end=3D"2849">
<p data-start=3D"2800" data-end=3D"2849"><code data-start=3D"2800" data-end=
=3D"2806">base</code> to 96 (or 128) if there=E2=80=99s still VRAM left.</p=
>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2850" data-end=3D"2927">
<p data-start=3D"2852" data-end=3D"2927">Aim for <strong data-start=3D"2860=
" data-end=3D"2886">30=E2=80=9340 epochs of precip</strong> in under a day =
=E2=80=94 very realistic on H100.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"2929" data-end=3D"2932">
<p data-start=3D"2934" data-end=3D"3149">If you tell me the <em data-start=
