</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<p data-start=3D"5407" data-end=3D"5476">Now all variables use the same str=
ong architecture and fresh weights.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"5478" data-end=3D"5481">
<h2 data-start=3D"5483" data-end=3D"5528">4=EF=B8=8F=E2=83=A3 Long term: ar=
chitecture vs small grids</h2>
<p data-start=3D"5530" data-end=3D"5702">You=E2=80=99re also right that <st=
rong data-start=3D"5553" data-end=3D"5573">training quality</strong> matter=
s.<br data-start=3D"5582" data-end=3D"5585">
But remember: those <strong data-start=3D"5605" data-end=3D"5637">shape mis=
match RuntimeErrors</strong> we saw are <strong data-start=3D"5649" data-en=
d=3D"5656">NOT</strong> caused by =E2=80=9Cbad training=E2=80=9D =E2=80=94 =
they=E2=80=99re caused by:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"5704" data-end=3D"5875">
<li data-start=3D"5704" data-end=3D"5755">
<p data-start=3D"5706" data-end=3D"5755">Downsampling many times inside the=
 big UNet, then</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5756" data-end=3D"5875">
<p data-start=3D"5758" data-end=3D"5875">Getting weird small grids from the=
 validator (like 6=C3=9715, 5=C3=9711, etc.), where one of the skip paths e=
nds up misaligned.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"5877" data-end=3D"5884">We did:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"5886" data-end=3D"5997">
<li data-start=3D"5886" data-end=3D"5933">
<p data-start=3D"5888" data-end=3D"5933">Spatial <strong data-start=3D"5896=
" data-end=3D"5907">padding</strong> before running the model,</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5934" data-end=3D"5958">
<p data-start=3D"5936" data-end=3D"5958">A <strong data-start=3D"5938" data=
-end=3D"5946">crop</strong> at the end,</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5959" data-end=3D"5997">
<p data-start=3D"5961" data-end=3D"5997">And now a <strong data-start=3D"59=
71" data-end=3D"5983">fallback</strong> in the miner.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"5999" data-end=3D"6186">That=E2=80=99s enough to stay safe=
 for now. Retraining u100/v100 will make their predictions <em data-start=
=3D"6084" data-end=3D"6092">better</em> when the model runs successfully, b=
ut the padding + fallback is what keeps you from crashing.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"6188" data-end=3D"6191">
<p data-start=3D"6193" data-end=3D"6233">If you want, next step after retra=
ining:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"6235" data-end=3D"6388">
<li data-start=3D"6235" data-end=3D"6388">
<p data-start=3D"6237" data-end=3D"6388">We can run a tiny <strong data-sta=
rt=3D"6255" data-end=3D"6271">offline_eval</strong> for u100 / v100 specifi=
cally, compare them to Open-Meteo baselines, and see how much they help you=
r effective score.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"6390" data-end=3D"6403">But in short:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"6405" data-end=3D"6824">
<li data-start=3D"6405" data-end=3D"6493">
<p data-start=3D"6407" data-end=3D"6493">You=E2=80=99re not crazy: wind mod=
els <em data-start=3D"6437" data-end=3D"6442">are</em> more fragile and pro=
bably need heavier training.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"6494" data-end=3D"6597">
<p data-start=3D"6496" data-end=3D"6597">The crashes you saw are mostly arc=
hitecture/shape quirks, which we=E2=80=99ve now wrapped with safety nets.</=
p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"6598" data-end=3D"6824">
<p data-start=3D"6600" data-end=3D"6824">Retraining <code data-start=3D"661=
1" data-end=3D"6637">100m_u_component_of_wind</code> and <code data-start=
=3D"6642" data-end=3D"6668">100m_v_component_of_wind</code> with the new <c=
ode data-start=3D"6682" data-end=3D"6703">train_model_unet.py</code> is a g=
ood idea and won=E2=80=99t break your miner =E2=80=94 it should only improv=
e how often it uses the real model versus the fallback.</p>
</li>
</ul>
