t w-full break-words light markdown-new-styling"><p data-start=3D"0" data-e=
nd=3D"86">Short answer: <strong data-start=3D"14" data-end=3D"84">no, just =
retraining the same architecture will not fix this error.</strong></p>
<p data-start=3D"88" data-end=3D"212">That =E2=80=9Csize of tensor a (6) mu=
st match size of tensor b (5)=E2=80=9D problem is <strong data-start=3D"159=
" data-end=3D"186">not about model quality</strong>, it=E2=80=99s about <st=
rong data-start=3D"199" data-end=3D"211">geometry</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"214" data-end=3D"494">
<li data-start=3D"214" data-end=3D"294">
<p data-start=3D"216" data-end=3D"294">The validator can send <strong data-=
start=3D"239" data-end=3D"256">any grid size</strong> (like <code data-star=
t=3D"263" data-end=3D"273">H=3D8, W=3D5</code>, <code data-start=3D"275" da=
ta-end=3D"286">H=3D14, W=3D9</code>, etc.).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"295" data-end=3D"494">
<p data-start=3D"297" data-end=3D"494">Your <strong data-start=3D"302" data=
-end=3D"315">deep UNet</strong> does multiple <code data-start=3D"330" data=
-end=3D"344">MaxPool2d(2)</code> =E2=86=92 spatial sizes go<br data-start=
=3D"363" data-end=3D"366">
14=E2=86=927=E2=86=923=E2=86=921=E2=86=92=E2=80=A6 or 5=E2=86=922=E2=86=921=
=E2=86=920,<br data-start=3D"390" data-end=3D"393">
and then the <strong data-start=3D"408" data-end=3D"436">upsampling + skip-=
concat</strong> tries to join tensors with sizes that don=E2=80=99t match =
=E2=86=92 boom.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"496" data-end=3D"693">You could train the 100m_u / 100m_v =
models for 1000 epochs and you=E2=80=99d still get shape-mismatch on those =
tiny / awkward grids, because the problem is <strong data-start=3D"644" dat=
a-end=3D"675">how the network changes H,W</strong>, not its weights.</p>
<p data-start=3D"695" data-end=3D"698">So:</p>
<h3 data-start=3D"700" data-end=3D"726">What actually fixes it</h3>
<p data-start=3D"728" data-end=3D"764">There are only three real solutions:=
</p>
<ol data-start=3D"766" data-end=3D"1626">
<li data-start=3D"766" data-end=3D"1035">
<p data-start=3D"769" data-end=3D"802"><strong data-start=3D"769" data-end=
=3D"802">Architecture change for winds</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"806" data-end=3D"1035">
<li data-start=3D"806" data-end=3D"896">
<p data-start=3D"808" data-end=3D"896">Use a <em data-start=3D"814" data-en=
d=3D"825">shallower</em> UNet for <code data-start=3D"835" data-end=3D"843"=
>100m_u</code> / <code data-start=3D"846" data-end=3D"854">100m_v</code> (e=
.g. only 2 downsamples instead of 4=E2=80=935).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"900" data-end=3D"953">
<p data-start=3D"902" data-end=3D"953">Then re-train those <strong data-sta=
rt=3D"922" data-end=3D"952">with that new smaller UNet</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"957" data-end=3D"1035">
<p data-start=3D"959" data-end=3D"1035">This reduces the chance that small =
grids collapse to 0=C3=97something internally.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1037" data-end=3D"1447">
<p data-start=3D"1040" data-end=3D"1100"><strong data-start=3D"1040" data-e=
nd=3D"1100">Runtime padding + cropping + fallback (what we=E2=80=99re doing=
)</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"1104" data-end=3D"1447">
<li data-start=3D"1104" data-end=3D"1336">
<p data-start=3D"1106" data-end=3D"1132">In <code data-start=3D"1109" data-=
end=3D"1131">model_loader.predict</code>:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"1138" data-end=3D"1336">
<li data-start=3D"1138" data-end=3D"1195">
<p data-start=3D"1140" data-end=3D"1195">Pad any <code data-start=3D"1148" =
data-end=3D"1153">H,W</code> up to a safe minimum (like 8=C3=978 or 16=C3=
=9716).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1201" data-end=3D"1251">
<p data-start=3D"1203" data-end=3D"1251">After model output, crop back to o=
riginal <code data-start=3D"1245" data-end=3D"1250">H,W</code>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1257" data-end=3D"1336">
<p data-start=3D"1259" data-end=3D"1336">If <em data-start=3D"1262" data-en=
d=3D"1272">anything</em> still throws, catch exception and use <strong data=
-start=3D"1311" data-end=3D"1335">persistence fallback</strong>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1340" data-end=3D"1447">
<p data-start=3D"1342" data-end=3D"1447">This guarantees: <em data-start=3D=
"1359" data-end=3D"1447">no matter what grid the validator sends, you alway=
s return something instead of crash.</em></p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1449" data-end=3D"1626">
<p data-start=3D"1452" data-end=3D"1490"><strong data-start=3D"1452" data-e=
nd=3D"1490">Skip UNet for winds on small grids</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"1494" data-end=3D"1626">
<li data-start=3D"1494" data-end=3D"1554">
<p data-start=3D"1496" data-end=3D"1554">If <code data-start=3D"1499" data-=
end=3D"1513">min(H,W) &lt; N</code> (like 8 or 12), <strong data-start=3D"1=
530" data-end=3D"1539">don=E2=80=99t</strong> call the UNet.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1558" data-end=3D"1626">
<p data-start=3D"1560" data-end=3D"1626">Just use persistence: repeat last =
context frame for that variable.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start=3D"1628" data-end=3D"1854">You already have (2)/(3) partially=
 in place. The remaining =E2=80=9Csize of tensor a=E2=80=A6=E2=80=9D errors=
 show up where the UNet is still being called on some small-ish grids <stro=
ng data-start=3D"1783" data-end=3D"1793">before</strong> the guard or with =
a model that downsamples too aggressively.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"1856" data-end=3D"1859">
<h3 data-start=3D"1861" data-end=3D"1893">So what should <em data-start=3D"=
1880" data-end=3D"1885">you</em> do now?</h3>
<p data-start=3D"1895" data-end=3D"1942">If you want it stable <strong data=
-start=3D"1917" data-end=3D"1924">and</strong> better for winds:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"1944" data-end=3D"2261">
<li data-start=3D"1944" data-end=3D"2033">
<p data-start=3D"1946" data-end=3D"2033">=E2=9C=85 Keep the <strong data-st=
art=3D"1957" data-end=3D"1975">fallback logic</strong> (persistence on erro=
r or tiny grids) =E2=80=93 this is mandatory.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2034" data-end=3D"2261">
<p data-start=3D"2036" data-end=3D"2126">=E2=9C=85 For <code data-start=3D"=
2042" data-end=3D"2050">100m_u</code> and <code data-start=3D"2055" data-en=
d=3D"2063">100m_v</code>, define a <strong data-start=3D"2074" data-end=3D"=
2090">smaller UNet</strong> (e.g. 3 levels instead of 5), then:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"2129" data-end=3D"2261">
<li data-start=3D"2129" data-end=3D"2185">
<p data-start=3D"2131" data-end=3D"2185">retrain only those two with that l=
ighter architecture;</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2188" data-end=3D"2261">
<p data-start=3D"2190" data-end=3D"2261">update <code data-start=3D"2197" d=
ata-end=3D"2211">model_loader</code> to load that special =E2=80=9Cwinds UN=
et=E2=80=9D for these vars.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"2263" data-end=3D"2333">That combination =3D<br data-start=
=3D"2281" data-end=3D"2284">
<strong data-start=3D"2284" data-end=3D"2332">no crashes + fewer fallbacks =
+ better scores</strong>.</p>
<p data-start=3D"2335" data-end=3D"2473">But purely =E2=80=9Ctrain again wi=
th the same big UNet=E2=80=9D =E2=86=92 the geometry bug stays, and the =E2=
=80=9Csize of tensor a/b must match=E2=80=9D error will still appear.</p>
